From da09c1fa0a8d8e5dd0614361be44ec8318b04b55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 17:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] extcon: sm5502: resolve coalesced attach/detach from the
 hardware state

sm5502_muic_irq_work() acts on the irq_attach and irq_detach flags in
sequence.  When both are set in the same run of the work item, which
happens whenever an attach and a detach interrupt are serviced in the
same batch, the attach handler publishes the cable state and the detach
handler immediately clears it again.  The extcon device then reports no
cable while one is plugged in, so a charger driver consuming the extcon
notifier falls back to its most conservative input current limit until
the next cable cycle.  On a Galaxy Tab A (SM-T580) DEV_TYPE1 reads 0x20
(USB SDP) while extcon reports USB=0 SDP=0 DCP=0.

When both flags are set, ask the hardware instead of replaying them:
sm5502_muic_get_cable_type() reads ADC and DEV_TYPE1 and returns a
negative value when no cable can be identified, so a coalesced pair
resolves to whatever is actually plugged in.

A lone flag is still taken at face value.  The work item runs some
milliseconds after the interrupt, and by then the chip may still report
the cable it has just lost: re-reading the hardware for a lone detach
would turn it into an attach, leaving for instance CDP=1 reported
indefinitely after the cable is pulled.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
---
 drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c b/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
index c8c4b9ef72..e10b05eb69 100644
--- a/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
+++ b/drivers/extcon/extcon-sm5502.c
@@ -531,16 +531,31 @@ static void sm5502_muic_irq_work(struct work_struct *work)
 
 	mutex_lock(&info->mutex);
 
-	/* Detect attached or detached cables */
-	if (info->irq_attach) {
+	/*
+	 * Detect attached or detached cables.
+	 *
+	 * Both flags can be set in the same run, in which case replaying them
+	 * in order would leave the state of whichever came last.  Ask the
+	 * hardware instead: a negative return means nothing identifiable is
+	 * connected.  A single flag is taken as it is, because by the time
+	 * this runs the chip may still be reporting a cable that has just
+	 * been unplugged.
+	 */
+	if (info->irq_attach && info->irq_detach) {
+		bool attached;
+
+		attached = sm5502_muic_get_cable_type(info) >= 0;
+
+		ret = sm5502_muic_cable_handler(info, attached);
+	} else if (info->irq_attach) {
 		ret = sm5502_muic_cable_handler(info, true);
-		info->irq_attach = false;
-	}
-	if (info->irq_detach) {
+	} else if (info->irq_detach) {
 		ret = sm5502_muic_cable_handler(info, false);
-		info->irq_detach = false;
 	}
 
+	info->irq_attach = false;
+	info->irq_detach = false;
+
 	if (ret < 0)
 		dev_err(info->dev, "failed to handle MUIC interrupt\n");
 
